WEBVTT

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.600
[upbeat music playing]

00:00:11.767 --> 00:00:14.400
[car engine revving]

00:00:19.186 --> 00:00:23.557
[Jerry] <i>Okay, okay, </i>
<i>this is my kind of car.</i>

00:00:23.633 --> 00:00:28.200
<i>This is a 1979 Porsche 930 Turbo</i>

00:00:28.267 --> 00:00:31.100
<i>in cashmere beige with a cork interior.</i>

00:00:33.667 --> 00:00:36.733
<i>It has a flat-6 3.3 liter engine</i>

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.067
<i>mounted at the extreme rear of the car,</i>

00:00:40.167 --> 00:00:44.367
<i>the worst possible place</i>
<i>for the most possible fun.</i>

00:00:45.045 --> 00:00:46.813
<i>It has a KKK Turbo,</i>

00:00:46.933 --> 00:00:49.767
<i>no relation to the racist</i>
<i>Southern Ku Klux Klan organization,</i>

00:00:49.833 --> 00:00:52.233
<i>giving it 265 horsepower.</i>

00:00:52.967 --> 00:00:55.133
<i>It has a four-speed manual gearbox.</i>

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:58.067
<i>It has cross-drilled,</i>
<i>ventilated disk brakes.</i>

00:00:58.133 --> 00:01:00.967
<i>It goes 162 miles an hour.</i>

00:01:01.033 --> 00:01:04.867
<i>This is a technical car for technical guys</i>
<i>who like technical things.</i>

00:01:06.433 --> 00:01:08.767
<i>The word "guys" in this usage</i>
<i>is an all-inclusive term</i>

00:01:08.833 --> 00:01:11.467
<i>for women, gays, </i>
<i>lesbians, and transgenders.</i>

00:01:11.533 --> 00:01:13.367
[upbeat music playing]

00:01:13.433 --> 00:01:16.533
<i>I love the color, I love the fenders,</i>
<i>I love the stance,</i>

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:18.867
<i>I love the accordion rubber bumpers,</i>

00:01:18.933 --> 00:01:21.567
<i>the upright cooling fan, the big tach,</i>

00:01:21.633 --> 00:01:24.900
<i>and the five-spoke Fuchs mag wheels.</i>

00:01:27.354 --> 00:01:32.059
<i>My very special guest today is</i>
<i>my dear old friend, Mr. Garry Shandling.</i>

00:01:33.167 --> 00:01:37.533
<i>Garry was one of the first comedian</i>
<i>friends of mine in LA to get a 9-11.</i>

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:40.233
<i>We have been very good friends</i>
<i>for a very long time,</i>

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:43.767
<i>and our careers have pretty much</i>
<i>paralleled most of that time.</i>

00:01:43.833 --> 00:01:46.800
<i>We started at The Comedy Store in LA</i>
<i>at the same time.</i>

00:01:46.867 --> 00:01:49.900
<i>We did</i> The Tonight Show
with Johnny Carson <i>at the same time.</i>

00:01:49.967 --> 00:01:54.167
<i>We did our TV series in the '90s</i>
<i>at the same lot, at the same time,</i>

00:01:54.233 --> 00:01:56.867
<i>right across the street from each other.</i>

00:01:56.933 --> 00:02:02.067
<i>We were a pair,</i>
<i>like 9-11s and Southern California.</i>

00:02:02.133 --> 00:02:03.800
[phone ringing]

00:02:03.867 --> 00:02:05.400
-[Jerry] <i>Garry.</i>
-[Garry] <i>Oh, Jerry!</i>

00:02:05.467 --> 00:02:07.933
<i>When do we do the phone call</i>
<i>that would be on air?</i>

00:02:08.033 --> 00:02:09.367
[Jerry] <i>Now. We're doing it now.</i>

00:02:09.433 --> 00:02:13.067
[Garry] <i>Oh, my God. I haven't talked to</i>
<i>you in so long, I can't wait to see you.</i>

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:17.433
<i>Maybe you had it right, because everything</i>
<i>in my life needs to be edited.</i>

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:21.500
[Jerry] <i>Well, everything in your life</i>
<i>comes after a 19 second pause.</i>

00:02:24.167 --> 00:02:26.100
[Garry] <i>I don't know why you say that.</i>

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:27.800
[both laughing]

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:33.133
<i>I'm Jerry Seinfeld, and this is</i>
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:37.633
-I love you.
-Love you, too.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:43.467
[upbeat music playing]

00:02:45.567 --> 00:02:47.733
Would you ever go into a tanning salon?

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:49.633
[Garry] Not without sunblock on.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:51.967
If they open a store,
I feel like I should go in.

00:02:52.005 --> 00:02:55.075
I think, "Oh, Burger Lounge.
Oh, I should go there and get a burger.

00:02:55.167 --> 00:02:57.433
They've gone to all the trouble
of opening that place up."

00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:00.967
I find you incredibly intelligent,
except for that idea,

00:03:01.033 --> 00:03:02.400
because you'd be busy all day.

00:03:02.467 --> 00:03:04.500
You would have nothing left in your life.

00:03:04.567 --> 00:03:06.467
-If we stopped everywhere...
-I would!

00:03:06.533 --> 00:03:07.467
...we'd have no show.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:11.933
[Garry] <i>The Matthew McConaughey </i>
<i>commercials for Lincoln,</i>

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.267
they could use a comedian
in the passenger seat.

00:03:14.333 --> 00:03:17.667
You should be in the passenger seat
looking at Matthew McConaughey.

00:03:17.733 --> 00:03:19.667
-Right.
-Saying, "Huh?

00:03:19.733 --> 00:03:21.467
I don't know what you're talking about.

00:03:21.567 --> 00:03:23.800
Are you trying to sell this car
to people who mumble?

00:03:23.867 --> 00:03:26.500
Do you want to sell it to people
who just kind of stumble in and go,

00:03:26.567 --> 00:03:30.867
'Hey, you know, I'm looking
for something like a Lincoln.'"

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:34.967
What is this? People driving around
by themselves just smirking.

00:03:35.033 --> 00:03:37.867
Who the hell goes from an Oscar
right to a car commercial?

00:03:37.933 --> 00:03:40.133
That's why you need to be
in the seat next to him.

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:42.433
That would be a great commercial.

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:45.300
You be Matthew McConaughey,
and I'll be you.

00:03:45.367 --> 00:03:46.933
[down-tempo music playing]

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:48.733
You just gotta drive.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.333
-It's Jerry. Oh, my God.
-[laughing]

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.500
I'm stuck in the car
with Matthew McConaughey.

00:03:55.933 --> 00:03:58.867
Did you find my jacket?
Did you find the leather jacket?

00:03:58.933 --> 00:04:00.133
Hold on one second.

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:03.067
I never thought about driving Lincolns,
I just drove them.

00:04:03.133 --> 00:04:05.167
It was a smart car, I just...

00:04:05.212 --> 00:04:09.549
This is so weird, he's not on the phone,
he's not talking to anybody, just listen.

00:04:09.633 --> 00:04:13.100
You got a straight flush, straight flush.

00:04:13.167 --> 00:04:15.933
[upbeat music playing]

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:17.933
-This is where Beatty lives, right?
-Yeah.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.800
Now it's suddenly turned into a star tour?

00:04:25.367 --> 00:04:29.300
So, it turns out I had
a hyperparathyroid gland

00:04:29.433 --> 00:04:32.633
that was undiagnosed,
because the symptoms mirrored

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:35.767
the exact same symptoms
an older Jewish man would have.

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:39.033
Which is, you know, lethargic,
you get puffy, you get heavy.

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:42.200
You kind of feel like you want a divorce,
even though you're not married.

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:48.000
You know, I was an electrical engineering
major at the University of Arizona,

00:04:48.067 --> 00:04:51.467
and that taught me the discipline
of working out formulas

00:04:51.533 --> 00:04:53.267
and having no other life,

00:04:53.467 --> 00:04:55.933
because you had to work so hard
to get that stuff right.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:59.067
But of course that was not as difficult
as writing comedy.

00:04:59.133 --> 00:05:03.800
No, because there's ultimately
an answer to an equation.

00:05:03.867 --> 00:05:05.167
There's an answer to a joke.

00:05:05.233 --> 00:05:07.700
Well, I don't think
Einstein bounced it off a crowd.

00:05:10.733 --> 00:05:14.633
What do you think of this?
A plus C equals D-squared. No?

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:16.433
-Yeah.
-That doesn't seem to get anything.

00:05:17.167 --> 00:05:20.133
[upbeat jazz music playing]

00:05:20.233 --> 00:05:23.533
[Garry] <i>There was a time </i>
<i>when I was writing for</i> Sanford and Son,

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:27.133
<i>and then walked into the Comedy Store,</i>
<i>and there you were doing standup.</i>

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:31.667
Then I was just starting amateur night.
Mitzi wouldn't give me any spots, really.

00:05:31.767 --> 00:05:34.033
[Jerry] <i>I worked at the Improv</i>
<i>because she disliked me.</i>

00:05:34.133 --> 00:05:35.833
<i>She just instinctively disliked me.</i>

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:38.400
Well, I can understand that.
I mean, really?

00:05:38.467 --> 00:05:39.733
[laughing]

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:43.567
-You and I were the two guys...
-Right.

00:05:43.633 --> 00:05:47.900
...who kind of had the notes, went in,
did the work, and left.

00:05:47.967 --> 00:05:51.733
And all that [bleep] that went on,
which we'll talk about when we get there,

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:55.000
because you'll still be able
to smell puke from 1978.

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:00.367
[Garry] <i>I'm not getting out. It's always</i>
<i>tough for me at The Comedy Store.</i>

00:06:00.433 --> 00:06:03.800
-There's a vibe that never changes.
-Never. It's horrible.

00:06:03.864 --> 00:06:05.165
But let's deal with it, come on.

00:06:06.867 --> 00:06:08.767
This is a little uncomfortable for me.

00:06:09.367 --> 00:06:13.633
But Garry,
this was the kingdom and the power.

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:16.733
<i>In the '70s and '80s,</i>
<i>this was the place you had to come to</i>

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:18.667
<i>if you wanted to be a real comedian.</i>

00:06:21.067 --> 00:06:25.367
[Jerry]<i> Did you ever see actual cocaine</i>
<i>or heroin at the Comedy Store?</i>

00:06:25.467 --> 00:06:27.633
-[Garry] <i>I did not.</i>
<i>-I didn't either,</i>

00:06:27.733 --> 00:06:29.400
<i>and we knew it was all around us.</i>

00:06:30.833 --> 00:06:35.067
But Garry,
I used to stand here and watch you.

00:06:35.133 --> 00:06:36.467
[Garry] This really is a killer.

00:06:36.533 --> 00:06:41.500
I go up on stage, and Jim McCawley from
<i>The Tonight Show</i> is standing back here.

00:06:41.567 --> 00:06:43.500
And he was the guardian of the gate.

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:45.133
So, Jim McCawley says to me

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:49.033
"That was so good. How's next Tuesday
for you on <i>The Tonight Show?"</i>

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:52.233
-And that's what changed my life.
-Wow.

00:06:52.345 --> 00:06:54.281
[pleasant jazz music playing]

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:03.167
[Garry] It's like every other person
has passed away.

00:07:03.233 --> 00:07:05.533
[Jerry] Either personally
or professionally passed away.

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:06.867
[Garry] That's right.

00:07:06.933 --> 00:07:07.967
[laughing]

00:07:09.829 --> 00:07:11.631
[inaudible]

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:15.700
You know what, Garry?
This is still a really good comedy room.

00:07:16.036 --> 00:07:18.004
I would love to go up and work out here.

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:21.500
And it's not just that we have
all the history here, this is a good room.

00:07:21.567 --> 00:07:24.733
You can feel it. Certain rooms
don't have it. This room has it.

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:26.000
[brisk jazz music playing]

00:07:26.413 --> 00:07:29.716
So, I'm on stage once. You know,
I'm talking and working on stuff,

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:32.000
and it's going fine, and I see movement.

00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:35.400
Out of the corner of my eye,
I see movement right over here.

00:07:35.588 --> 00:07:41.895
And I look down,
and a new waitress has got her tray,

00:07:42.333 --> 00:07:47.400
and she's come up this way onto the stage
to serve these two tables,

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:52.100
and I'm actually standing there like this.

00:07:52.167 --> 00:07:55.800
I'm not mad,
I just can't believe what I'm seeing.

00:07:55.867 --> 00:07:58.933
So, people say, "Wow, do you get nervous
when you go on TV?"

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:01.767
They don't understand
the [bleep] we've seen.

00:08:02.767 --> 00:08:06.533
One night, I was here,
and it must have been 1:00 in the morning,

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:08.233
and the guy was sitting right there.

00:08:08.467 --> 00:08:10.633
And there were no...
There was nobody else here,

00:08:10.857 --> 00:08:12.392
and I just started up on him.

00:08:12.467 --> 00:08:13.933
[laughing]

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:16.700
But here's what happened.
I said, "You know what? I feel so bad.

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:20.900
I always pick on the person
that's in front of the mic, so just move."

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:23.933
So, he was a nice guy,
and he got up and he moved over there.

00:08:24.633 --> 00:08:29.733
And I picked up the mic and I put it here,
and I started up on him again.

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:31.733
[both laughing]

00:08:32.833 --> 00:08:34.933
[slow jazz music playing]

00:08:40.533 --> 00:08:43.433
It still has its vibe.
I mean, I didn't really know how to be.

00:08:43.533 --> 00:08:45.933
-I was a guy from Arizona that came--
-[Jerry] So what?

00:08:47.867 --> 00:08:49.800
Nobody gives a [bleep] where you're from.

00:08:51.264 --> 00:08:53.133
Yeah, I'm sorry I brought that up.

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:02.333
-Another big Garry shot. Look at that one.
-This picture's totally embarrassing.

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:04.267
-You look so handsome.
-Where's your picture?

00:09:04.367 --> 00:09:05.500
Don't know, I didn't see it.

00:09:05.567 --> 00:09:07.400
Just skipping it
because you don't want to--

00:09:07.467 --> 00:09:09.800
I didn't see it. I looked for it,
I didn't see it.

00:09:10.517 --> 00:09:12.452
I'm really okay. I'm okay with it.

00:09:12.519 --> 00:09:13.920
[inaudible]

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:15.600
What's that doing up there?

00:09:16.056 --> 00:09:19.325
[Garry] I think that's if you're laying
flat in the parking lot,

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:20.700
and you think your life is over,

00:09:20.767 --> 00:09:24.400
you look up and you go, "Wait,
there's another workshop I could go to."

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:26.767
[upbeat jazz music playing]

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:31.500
[Jerry] You know, David Brenner
passed away last year.

00:09:31.567 --> 00:09:34.867
Do you ever think about,
like, all that material?

00:09:35.208 --> 00:09:36.309
I'm sorry.

00:09:36.867 --> 00:09:39.967
So, I'm at a stage in my life
where I actually care about the person.

00:09:40.033 --> 00:09:41.233
Here's what I thought you were gonna say.

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:43.367
"Did you ever realize
when David Brenner died,

00:09:43.450 --> 00:09:46.019
and Robin,
the actual impermanence of life?"

00:09:46.086 --> 00:09:48.555
I never thought,
"God, there goes a lot of material."

00:09:48.621 --> 00:09:51.858
-That's hilarious that you think that way.
-[Jerry] Well, that was the hard part.

00:09:51.933 --> 00:09:54.333
Anyone can just pop out and live,
that's easy.

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:56.267
Gee, all that material,
he worked so hard on it.

00:09:56.333 --> 00:09:58.867
It's just gone. It doesn't mean anything
to anyone anymore.

00:09:58.967 --> 00:10:00.633
I mean, it took so much work to create it.

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:03.200
That material, and your material,

00:10:03.267 --> 00:10:07.467
is purely a vehicle for you
to express your spirit.

00:10:07.667 --> 00:10:10.933
And your soul and your being, and that's
why you're fantastic, so you keep...

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:13.033
So it doesn't have any value beyond that?

00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:18.233
It doesn't have any value
beyond you expressing yourself spiritually

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:20.833
in a very soulful, spiritual way.

00:10:20.900 --> 00:10:23.067
It's why you're on the planet.

00:10:23.256 --> 00:10:27.127
God, open up the sunroof.
What year is this?

00:10:27.233 --> 00:10:29.700
So, when you saw Robin Williams
the first time,

00:10:29.767 --> 00:10:33.167
you don't remember everything he said,
it was just that, his presence.

00:10:33.233 --> 00:10:36.100
[slow jazz music playing]

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:42.200
May Robin rest in peace.
I'm sure, like me, you were stunned.

00:10:42.509 --> 00:10:44.210
-I don't know how you felt.
-Yeah. Mmm-hmm.

00:10:44.300 --> 00:10:46.900
I was sitting there watching CNN anyway,

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:50.000
and they broke in and said,
"Robin Williams killed himself."

00:10:50.100 --> 00:10:51.267
I sat there, and I was frozen.

00:10:51.367 --> 00:10:53.533
This is totally true,
and I was in that kitchen of mine.

00:10:53.867 --> 00:10:57.233
Then Wolf Blitzer says, "63 is so young."

00:10:57.700 --> 00:10:59.300
And then I looked up with a little hope,

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:02.067
because I'm about the same age
as Robin, all right?

00:11:02.133 --> 00:11:04.300
Then I realized "63 is so young"

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:07.467
is a phrase you never hear
relative to anything but death.

00:11:07.533 --> 00:11:09.233
-Right.
-"Sixty-three is so young

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:12.233
to be playing in the NFL."
You don't... Nothing.

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:16.667
You have to die in your 60s for them
to say, "Boy, he was young."

00:11:16.733 --> 00:11:19.367
[cheerful music playing]

00:11:22.833 --> 00:11:26.300
I forgot to ask her for coffee right away.
Are you in the mood for coffee?

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:27.867
Yeah, I can do more coffee.

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:35.333
[Jerry] <i>In the old days, you and I,</i>

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:38.933
we were both handling
these very challenging jobs that we had.

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:41.400
I ran into somebody
whose name I can't recall.

00:11:41.467 --> 00:11:44.200
Some comedian who had
a 12-show commitment.

00:11:44.267 --> 00:11:47.600
I said, "How's it going?" He said,
"This is so much easier than I thought.

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:49.300
I'm having so much fun." And I thought...

00:11:49.976 --> 00:11:52.545
-Oh, my God.
-Oh, my God. He's in trouble.

00:11:52.633 --> 00:11:54.367
-And then, of course, he was out.
-He's out.

00:11:54.433 --> 00:11:58.733
But, in any case, we're really connecting
four layers below this cup of coffee.

00:11:58.800 --> 00:11:59.633
Yes.

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:02.433
This is part of the key
of our relationship.

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:04.400
Because when I say,
"It's a [bleep] minefield,"

00:12:04.467 --> 00:12:06.000
they go, "Shandling's complaining."

00:12:06.226 --> 00:12:10.029
You know, I listen to the blues,
it's the exact same words I say,

00:12:10.467 --> 00:12:12.833
and people go, "This is fantastic."

00:12:13.066 --> 00:12:16.536
But when I say, "My baby left me,"
they go, "Stop complaining."

00:12:16.633 --> 00:12:19.700
[slow jazz music playing]

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:24.967
I noticed that I go through
a reflective period,

00:12:25.033 --> 00:12:27.033
and think about either some relationship

00:12:27.100 --> 00:12:31.733
I might have been in at the time,
or my exploits at the Carlisle.

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:34.533
You have exploits?
I can't believe you have exploits.

00:12:36.767 --> 00:12:38.500
-What? Come on.
-Nothing.

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:40.967
-I can't, I can't right now. I just can't.
-All right.

00:12:41.033 --> 00:12:42.433
Suit yourself.

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:48.300
There's a little attitude
when you say "Suit yourself" there.

00:12:48.367 --> 00:12:50.967
I like to say "Suit yourself"
because nobody says that anymore.

00:12:51.033 --> 00:12:52.633
I like "Go [bleep] yourself."

00:12:52.700 --> 00:12:54.000
[both laugh]

00:12:54.067 --> 00:12:55.433
-Why?
-I don't know.

00:12:55.500 --> 00:12:57.800
That's another expression that just...

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:00.467
There's no image
even that it takes you to.

00:13:00.533 --> 00:13:03.000
I don't know
why that line became so popular.

00:13:03.067 --> 00:13:04.667
-Which?
-"Go [bleep] yourself."

00:13:04.733 --> 00:13:07.567
That's the way I actually initiate sex.

00:13:07.667 --> 00:13:09.000
[both laughing]

00:13:09.867 --> 00:13:10.967
That's my opening.

00:13:11.567 --> 00:13:13.967
[jazz music playing]

00:13:15.295 --> 00:13:16.963
[Jerry] <i>So, when you did </i>Larry Sanders,

00:13:17.033 --> 00:13:19.967
which besides being
one of my favorite sitcoms of all time,

00:13:20.067 --> 00:13:24.633
you invented a certain kind of air on TV
that had never existed before.

00:13:24.700 --> 00:13:26.833
There's a certain kind of air
on <i>I Love Lucy,</i>

00:13:26.900 --> 00:13:29.267
there's a certain kind of air
on <i>Mary Tyler Moore.</i>

00:13:29.333 --> 00:13:32.433
You created an original content form.

00:13:32.500 --> 00:13:35.167
Do you ever watch TV,
<i>The Office, Modern Family,</i>

00:13:35.233 --> 00:13:39.033
and go, "Oh, look, they're still doing me.
Still doing that thing I invented."

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:42.033
I knew when I explained to HBO
what I wanted to do,

00:13:42.100 --> 00:13:44.700
I couldn't say, "It's a little bit
of this, a little bit of that."

00:13:44.767 --> 00:13:48.333
I couldn't even do that.
And I mean, they thought I was crazy.

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:50.467
I said, "This doesn't even exist.

00:13:50.667 --> 00:13:53.800
The world doesn't even exist.
What are you doing?"

00:13:53.867 --> 00:13:56.167
-Who would you say this to?
-To other homeless people.

00:13:57.500 --> 00:13:59.933
[jazz music playing]

00:14:01.767 --> 00:14:02.933
About eight months ago,

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:04.833
I'm home sick, I was sick.

00:14:04.900 --> 00:14:08.800
When a Jew doesn't feel well,
he cannot repeat it too many times.

00:14:09.015 --> 00:14:12.852
Even the Buddhist part of me
was saying, "You're sick."

00:14:13.086 --> 00:14:15.988
Melissa Rivers had a memorial for her mom,

00:14:16.289 --> 00:14:19.192
-and I was not well.
-I understand.

00:14:19.267 --> 00:14:22.600
So, I got home at, like, 10:30 p.m.,
and I was exhausted.

00:14:22.667 --> 00:14:25.367
I'd just gone through that,
and I'm taking my suit off

00:14:25.433 --> 00:14:27.900
like those scenes where you see
someone's taking their clothes off

00:14:28.067 --> 00:14:30.667
leading to a sexual scene,
except it's just menswear.

00:14:30.733 --> 00:14:32.100
It's just my clothing.

00:14:32.167 --> 00:14:34.367
I get into bed and I'm exhausted,

00:14:34.433 --> 00:14:36.600
because I was not feeling well,
and I'm exhausted.

00:14:36.667 --> 00:14:37.567
Yeah, you mentioned that.

00:14:37.667 --> 00:14:40.333
And I get in bed, and it's going on 11:30,

00:14:40.800 --> 00:14:42.300
and on <i>Jimmy Fallon...</i>

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:44.533
they announce Jeffrey Tambor
is on <i>Jimmy Fallon.</i>

00:14:44.600 --> 00:14:46.467
So I go, "I gotta stay up and watch this."

00:14:46.533 --> 00:14:49.800
And I'm watching it,
and I'm not feeling well.

00:14:49.867 --> 00:14:50.967
You mentioned that.

00:14:51.033 --> 00:14:54.100
And then Jimmy says to Jeffrey,
"How's Garry?"

00:14:54.667 --> 00:14:56.667
And he said,
"He's probably watching right now."

00:14:56.733 --> 00:15:00.100
And they turned to the camera,
and they go, "Hi, Garry."

00:15:00.167 --> 00:15:02.600
And I'm sitting there, like this.

00:15:02.833 --> 00:15:05.600
"Hey, Garry, how you doing?"
It was like, "Hope you're feeling okay."

00:15:05.700 --> 00:15:08.800
I mean, they don't know I'm sick
or anything. It startled me.

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:10.933
I was a little close to the TV.

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:15.967
And so I wrote Jimmy a note that just
said, "Please, don't ever do that again."

00:15:16.033 --> 00:15:17.000
[laughing]

00:15:20.133 --> 00:15:25.767
What I want at my funeral is an actual
boxing referee to do a count,

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:29.267
and at five, just wave it off and say,
"He's not getting up."

00:15:29.767 --> 00:15:31.100
[both laughing]

00:15:31.931 --> 00:15:33.900
[pleasant jazz music playing]

00:15:33.966 --> 00:15:35.902
[inaudible]

00:15:38.237 --> 00:15:41.007
-Did you use to come out here...
-No, I never walked.

00:15:41.100 --> 00:15:43.133
-...and go to restaurants?
-No, the trouble is,

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:48.733
I was so busy. I remember going out to eat
once with a guest who was on the show,

00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:51.367
and when I got back 30 minutes later,

00:15:51.633 --> 00:15:54.800
our producer was standing
in my parking spot like this.

00:15:55.521 --> 00:15:58.825
And, you know, I'm gonna say this now.
It was my show.

00:15:58.900 --> 00:16:01.267
-Right.
-Can I just have one lunch?

00:16:01.333 --> 00:16:04.300
-You want me to answer that question? No.
-Yes. Yeah.

00:16:04.367 --> 00:16:06.467
-Get your ass back in the office.
-Yeah.

00:16:06.532 --> 00:16:10.102
Nobody wants to hear your whining
about having your own show...

00:16:10.169 --> 00:16:11.904
-All the money you've made...
-[Garry] Right.

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:15.767
[Jerry] Nobody likes to hear rich, famous
people whining about how hard it was.

00:16:15.833 --> 00:16:18.600
[Garry] Well, then we sure don't have
a presidential candidate, do we?

00:16:23.115 --> 00:16:24.851
-[Jerry] How you doing?
-[guard] All right, you?

00:16:24.933 --> 00:16:27.800
I'm with Garry Shandling.
We used to work here.

00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:30.833
-Really? Vaguely.
-Do you remember us?

00:16:32.967 --> 00:16:35.467
This is those parking spots
for your offices.

00:16:35.533 --> 00:16:36.600
Yeah, we were in here.

00:16:36.829 --> 00:16:38.297
[Jerry] I remember one of my writers

00:16:38.364 --> 00:16:40.032
told me they talked
to one of your writers,

00:16:40.100 --> 00:16:41.800
and they said
that they came to your house,

00:16:41.867 --> 00:16:44.633
and that you would float in the pool,
and they'd be pitching.

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:49.033
If you said to me, I was face down
in the pool, I'd say, "That's possible."

00:16:49.100 --> 00:16:51.267
[gentle jazz music playing]

00:16:53.579 --> 00:16:57.550
[Jerry] This is the walk that we would do.
Many times we would call each other.

00:16:57.617 --> 00:16:58.651
We'd say, "Got a minute?"

00:16:58.733 --> 00:17:01.667
And we would walk
and hear each other's issues.

00:17:01.733 --> 00:17:05.133
In fact, I've said,
"Thank God I could speak to Jerry."

00:17:05.200 --> 00:17:06.333
Here's your New York street.

00:17:06.400 --> 00:17:08.933
[Jerry] Yeah, this street
was built for me and my show.

00:17:09.228 --> 00:17:10.563
[both] Wow!

00:17:10.633 --> 00:17:12.067
[Garry laughing]

00:17:12.433 --> 00:17:13.267
Whoo.

00:17:13.367 --> 00:17:16.500
I have not been here in many years.

00:17:16.536 --> 00:17:18.104
[inaudible]

00:17:18.200 --> 00:17:20.800
[gentle jazz music playing]

00:17:26.100 --> 00:17:28.600
-I got two jokes in one episode.
-[Jerry] Yeah, you did.

00:17:28.667 --> 00:17:32.333
It was the one where George took the book
at the bookstore into the bathroom.

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:33.333
That's right.

00:17:33.400 --> 00:17:36.933
George says to me, "Well, don't you
read when you go in the bathroom?"

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:40.100
And I go, "No, George, I don't.
I just use the bathroom."

00:17:40.167 --> 00:17:44.967
And George's line was,
"Well, aren't you something?"

00:17:45.700 --> 00:17:46.700
That was your line.

00:17:50.536 --> 00:17:52.004
[Jerry whispers] The script girl walk.

00:17:52.133 --> 00:17:54.900
The brisk, high-energy script girl walk.

00:17:54.967 --> 00:17:57.233
It's another world, isn't it?
Another time?

00:17:57.333 --> 00:17:59.167
-Yeah.
-[Garry] What is your feeling?

00:17:59.233 --> 00:18:01.133
Positive? Do you miss it?

00:18:01.200 --> 00:18:03.267
[Jerry whispers] Do you remember
when you were a kid,

00:18:03.316 --> 00:18:08.054
and you were going to the bathroom
at school, and you could pee so far?

00:18:08.120 --> 00:18:10.356
You could just, you would step back
further and further

00:18:10.433 --> 00:18:12.767
and you could pee, like, 12 feet.

00:18:13.426 --> 00:18:14.694
It's like that.

00:18:15.361 --> 00:18:17.964
-[Jerry chuckling]
-I didn't know how to put it.

00:18:22.234 --> 00:18:23.769
[Garry] This feels like it's almost...

00:18:23.836 --> 00:18:25.871
It's not your set,
but it's the same dimension.

00:18:25.972 --> 00:18:29.609
[Jerry] Yes, it is, it's very similar.
I bet you we could still do a sitcom.

00:18:29.709 --> 00:18:31.744
All you need is an awkward situation.

00:18:32.033 --> 00:18:34.533
[upbeat jazz music playing]

00:18:35.181 --> 00:18:37.416
[whispering] What did you do,
did you drink everything?

00:18:38.284 --> 00:18:40.653
[scoffs, whispers]
Now what am I gonna do?

00:18:40.767 --> 00:18:42.533
We can't both go out with her.

00:18:42.900 --> 00:18:44.867
If you asked her out and I asked her out.

00:18:44.933 --> 00:18:46.933
Now I'm gonna have to go out to eat.

00:18:47.500 --> 00:18:49.400
What if I go out with her,
then I pretend I'm sick,

00:18:49.467 --> 00:18:51.433
I'll leave in the middle of the date
and then you show up.

00:18:51.500 --> 00:18:53.300
She'll never notice the difference.

00:18:53.367 --> 00:18:54.767
You know, she isn't that smart.

00:18:54.834 --> 00:18:57.570
It's been really on my mind
to talk you about that.

00:18:59.200 --> 00:19:00.100
Thanks.

00:19:00.167 --> 00:19:02.033
[Jerry, softly] We used to run this town.

00:19:02.100 --> 00:19:04.633
[upbeat music playing]

00:19:07.380 --> 00:19:10.916
[Garry] Remember, there was a blind
comedian named Gabriel something,

00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:14.433
and I wrote him a joke about, he drives
with his hands out the car window,

00:19:14.533 --> 00:19:18.133
because those little bumps
on the lines are actually Braille markers.

00:19:18.200 --> 00:19:22.100
What makes that joke funny is that
it's not about a blind guy driving.

00:19:22.167 --> 00:19:24.200
I wrote a suicide note once that said,

00:19:24.267 --> 00:19:28.000
"I'm not mad at anyone. This is just
something I want to do for myself."

00:19:28.067 --> 00:19:30.367
It's not funny, is it?
It's too close to the truth.

00:19:30.433 --> 00:19:31.500
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:19:31.567 --> 00:19:33.567
[Jerry] So, being completely honest.

00:19:33.633 --> 00:19:36.400
The shock of Robin Williams
committing suicide.

00:19:36.467 --> 00:19:38.533
Were you completely surprised?

00:19:38.600 --> 00:19:41.867
The news broadcasters were on TV saying

00:19:42.048 --> 00:19:44.750
"Why would Robin Williams
want to kill himself?

00:19:44.950 --> 00:19:47.453
He just finished doing a TV series,

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:49.555
he's got two movies in the can,

00:19:49.633 --> 00:19:51.433
and he's scheduled to shoot another one.

00:19:51.500 --> 00:19:53.600
Why would you kill yourself?"

00:19:53.826 --> 00:19:58.264
And I was sitting there with somebody,
I said, "She just gave the reason."

00:19:58.333 --> 00:20:00.967
[slow jazz music playing]

00:20:01.867 --> 00:20:04.333
[Garry] So, one time I was
with the Dalai Lama and he said,

00:20:04.400 --> 00:20:06.433
"I understand you have some joke
about the Buddha."

00:20:06.500 --> 00:20:09.300
How would you like to be in this position?
"Your Holiness."

00:20:09.967 --> 00:20:12.933
And you know, he's looking at me, he's got
the glasses on, he's the Dalai Lama.

00:20:13.033 --> 00:20:16.100
I'm talking to the Dalai Lama.
And I'm comfortable with that.

00:20:16.348 --> 00:20:19.318
And I said, "Well, Buddha never got
married because his wife would have said,

00:20:19.433 --> 00:20:21.767
"'Are you gonna sit around
the house like that all day?'

00:20:21.833 --> 00:20:23.067
'Well, I'm meditating, honey.'

00:20:23.133 --> 00:20:25.567
'Why don't you meditate
while you're taking out the trash?'"

00:20:26.000 --> 00:20:28.400
And there's dead silence
in this little hut,

00:20:28.500 --> 00:20:30.800
except for the three American monks

00:20:30.900 --> 00:20:33.500
who are laughing hysterically.

00:20:33.567 --> 00:20:35.767
And so, then I explained to him the joke.

00:20:35.901 --> 00:20:39.705
"In American culture,
the husbands and wives sometimes argue,

00:20:39.772 --> 00:20:42.842
because the wife thinks
the husband is lazy and sitting around."

00:20:42.967 --> 00:20:44.633
Now he goes, "Oh!

00:20:45.767 --> 00:20:46.867
Funny."

00:20:47.012 --> 00:20:49.548
That's what you want to do,
you want to have a three-minute gap

00:20:49.615 --> 00:20:51.917
from when you tell the joke
to when you get the laugh.

00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:53.433
[Jerry laughing]

00:20:59.533 --> 00:21:02.300
You know, I went in to get a CT scan
last Wednesday.

00:21:02.528 --> 00:21:05.297
And I go in there, and the guy says,
the tech guy,

00:21:05.367 --> 00:21:07.500
"Hey, Garry Shandling, I'm a big fan."

00:21:07.600 --> 00:21:10.600
He said, "I did a CT scan
a year ago of you."

00:21:10.767 --> 00:21:13.167
He said, "Do you have cancer?"
I said, "No."

00:21:13.233 --> 00:21:15.833
He said, "Good, you're still alive.
'Cause I was watching the news,

00:21:15.900 --> 00:21:19.133
and it seems like if you had passed away
I would have heard about it on the news."

00:21:19.745 --> 00:21:21.614
And I said, "Well, I don't know, man.

00:21:21.680 --> 00:21:25.217
I mean, uh, I don't know if they would
have broken in or anything, but..."

00:21:25.451 --> 00:21:27.019
I didn't know what to say to the guy.

00:21:27.100 --> 00:21:30.233
He kept at it. And he said, "That's so
great that Garry Shandling is still alive.

00:21:30.300 --> 00:21:32.267
Fantastic." But it was like...